waza data_21() -> File { kaesu File { path: "kiso/aot/lower/nativefs/reactor_linux.mio", version: "Bunko-v2.1.1", length: 13205, sha256: "5ac4a0fc2e655c83ac3fc1887128cb12a1827ba3c188784d6b1c199cde0e6756", source: "yobu \"kiso/aot/arm64\";\nyobu \"kiso/convert\";\nyobu \"kiso/string\";\n\n// Linux AArch64 reactor (group M): epoll backend of the mio/sys/socket reactor primitives, emitted when the lowering targets Linux\n// (abi 1). These routines call the Linux kernel directly (`svc #0`, number in x8, negative errno in x0), unlike the rest of the\n// NativeFS code, which speaks Darwin numbers to the linux_syscall adapter: epoll has no Darwin counterpart. A failure is converted to\n// the Darwin errno the shared classifier expects with the adapter's lx_errno. Linux AArch64 numbers: eventfd2 19, epoll_create1 20,\n// epoll_ctl 21, epoll_pwait 22, read 63, write 64. Unlike x86-64, struct epoll_event is not packed here: events (u32) at 0, data (u64) at 8.\n// Level-triggered, the same contract as the x86-64 and kqueue backends: wake-up through an eventfd registered with the epoll instance.\n\nwaza lrx(code: arm64.Assembler, value: arm64.Instruction) -> unit { emit(code, value); }\nwaza lrb(code: arm64.Assembler, target: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_b(code, target); }\nwaza lrc(code: arm64.Assembler, condition: int64, target: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_b_cond(code, condition, target); }\nwaza lrz(code: arm64.Assembler, reg: int64, target: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_cbz_x(code, reg, target); }\nwaza lrnz(code: arm64.Assembler, reg: int64, target: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_cbnz_x(code, reg, target); }\nwaza lrl(code: arm64.Assembler, target: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_bl(code, target); }\nwaza lrlabel(code: arm64.Assembler, name: string) -> unit { arm64.assembler_label(code, name); }\n\n// After `svc #0`: a non-negative x0 continues at `ok`; a negative errno becomes the positive Darwin errno in x0 and continues at `error`.\nwaza linux_result