ed.bytes, error: \"\", externals: assembled.externals, bss_size: bss };\n}\n" }; }
waza data_40() -> File { kaesu File { path: "kiso/aot/lowerlinux/maps.mio", version: "Bunko-v2.1.1", length: 16096, sha256: "6d4764324198c7a0d5f0677cfabe426e5f1c29d32e44c5745d5c7af57fabafeb", source: "yobu \"kiso/aot/lower/types\";\nyobu \"kiso/aot/x86_64\";\n\n// Map[K, V] with integer, bool or string keys (the key kinds the language allows and the reference backend supports).\n// Map object (kind 12, 32 bytes): [16] bucket table (0 until the first insert), [24] entry count. Bucket table (kind 14, 24 + 8n bytes):\n// [16] n (a power of two, at least 8), then n bucket heads. Entry (kind 13, 48 bytes): [16] next in its bucket, [24] key, [32] value,\n// [40] flags (bit 0: the key is a reference (string), bit 1: the value is a reference, bit 2: the value is nil) with the 56-bit key hash\n// in bits 8..63. A new key is pushed at the front of its bucket and an existing key is updated in place; the table doubles when the\n// count reaches n, relinking the entries by their stored hash (no key is rehashed, nothing is copied). The collector marks the table\n// from the map and every bucket head from the table; it reads only bits 0 and 1 of the flags word. Runtime routines never collect, so a\n// resize is never observed half done. Map has no iteration, deletion or length operation, so bucket order is not observable.\n// Values are stored raw: a nullable scalar is unboxed on the way in and boxed again on the way out. A nil nullable scalar is stored as\n// raw 0 with bit 2 set, so a stored nil is distinguishable from a stored zero and the entry (key presence) never depends on the payload.\n//\n// rt.map_new -> rax = map\n// rt.map_hash: rsi = key, rdx = 1 for string keys -> rax = 56-bit hash (clobbers rcx, rdx, r8, r9)\n// rt.map_find: rdi = map, rsi = key, rdx = 1 for string keys -> rax = entry or 0, rdx = key hash\n// rt.map_set: rdi = map, rsi = key, rdx = value, rcx = flags (bit 0 string key, bit 1 reference value, bit 2 nil value)\nwaza emit_map_runtime(code: x86_64.Assem